WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.336
This video covers safe over the air updates with automatic rollback, and the testing and CI strategy a senior engineer is expected to own.

00:00:08.736 --> 00:00:17.919
OTA: Over-the-air update: new software delivered to devices in the field over the network. Like: A software home delivery, no service visit needed.

00:00:18.219 --> 00:00:30.282
CI / HIL: Continuous integration runs tests on every change; hardware-in-the-loop (HIL) runs them on real boards. Like: A quality inspector at every station of the assembly line, some with real parts on the bench.

00:00:31.382 --> 00:00:35.446
Tonight ten thousand bars in customer offices will install new firmware.

00:00:35.696 --> 00:00:42.371
Firmware: Software that runs close to hardware, usually stored in flash memory on the device. Like: The instincts built into an animal: low-level behaviour that is there from birth.

00:00:42.621 --> 00:00:47.231
If one in a thousand fails to boot, that's ten angry customers by morning.

00:00:47.481 --> 00:00:51.045
Let's see how updates and tests make that number zero.

00:00:52.095 --> 00:00:56.356
Updating firmware in the field is like replacing an airplane engine mid flight.

00:00:56.756 --> 00:00:59.449
Keep one good engine running. That's slot A.

00:00:59.849 --> 00:01:03.576
Install the new engine on the other side. That's slot B.

00:01:03.976 --> 00:01:05.543
Test it before you trust it.

00:01:05.943 --> 00:01:08.730
And if it coughs, switch back automatically.

00:01:09.930 --> 00:01:10.626
Words.

00:01:11.026 --> 00:01:16.030
An A B update keeps two copies of each partition, and writes only the inactive one.

00:01:16.430 --> 00:01:27.692
A/B partitions: Two copies of the system; update the idle one and switch, falling back if the new one fails. Like: Two identical stages: rehearse on one while the show runs on the other.

00:01:27.992 --> 00:01:31.463
A boot counter tracks how many tries the new slot has left.

00:01:31.863 --> 00:01:35.474
Anti rollback refuses images older than a stored version.

00:01:35.874 --> 00:01:39.763
Hardware in the loop means automated tests on real boards in a rack.

00:01:40.163 --> 00:01:43.797
And bisecting is a binary search through commits to find a regression.

00:01:44.997 --> 00:01:46.959
An update, step by step.

00:01:47.359 --> 00:01:53.037
The agent writes the new image into the inactive slot, B. The device keeps running from A.

00:01:53.437 --> 00:01:58.824
It verifies the hash, the signature, and the version against the anti rollback counter.

00:01:59.224 --> 00:02:01.987
It marks B active, with three tries.

00:02:02.387 --> 00:02:03.931
The bootloader boots B.

00:02:04.331 --> 00:02:10.949
Bootloader: The small program that runs first at power-on, sets up memory and loads the kernel. Like: The stagehand who sets up the stage before the actors arrive.

00:02:11.199 --> 00:02:15.448
But B fails its health checks, so it never marks itself successful.

00:02:15.848 --> 00:02:19.888
The bootloader retries, decrementing the counter, until it reaches zero.

00:02:20.288 --> 00:02:21.995
Then it falls back to A.

00:02:22.395 --> 00:02:32.705
And A reports the failure, so the team sees it in telemetry. Mark a slot successful only after real health checks: services up, USB enumerated, camera streaming.

00:02:33.105 --> 00:02:40.349
Enumeration: The first conversation after plug-in where the laptop learns what the device is and gives it an address. Like: A new employee's first morning: show ID, get a badge number, hand in a resume, get assigned a desk.

00:02:41.399 --> 00:02:42.758
Now the test pyramid.

00:02:43.158 --> 00:02:51.412
At the base, unit tests on a PC with sanitizers, like labs four, five, eight, nine and ten. Seconds, on every commit.

00:02:51.812 --> 00:03:01.065
Then virtual tests: QEMU, dummy h c d, vivid and v four l two compliance, like our lab zero harness. Minutes, on every merge.

00:03:01.465 --> 00:03:09.778
QEMU: A program that emulates a whole computer, so you can test kernels without real hardware. Like: A flight simulator for software.

00:03:10.078 --> 00:03:20.179
V4L2: Video for Linux 2: the kernel interface every Linux camera and video device uses. Like: The standard loading dock every camera truck backs into.

00:03:20.479 --> 00:03:32.634
Then a hardware in the loop rack: real boards, programmable USB switches, HDMI generators, and power relays. Enumerate a thousand times on three host operating systems, every night.

00:03:33.034 --> 00:03:41.487
HDMI: The common cable standard for sending video and audio to TVs and monitors. Like: A garden hose for pictures and sound.

00:03:41.787 --> 00:03:48.184
Then certification: Teams and Zoom, USB I F compliance, and Android's test suites.

00:03:48.584 --> 00:03:54.354
And field telemetry: crashes, enumeration failures and frame drops from real rooms.

00:03:55.554 --> 00:03:59.977
On every merge, run fast checks, like parsing the golden descriptor dump.

00:04:00.377 --> 00:04:03.616
Descriptor: A small table of facts the device reports about itself: who made it, what it can do. Like: A business card plus resume the device hands over when it is first plugged in.

00:04:03.866 --> 00:04:05.492
Then run the QEMU harness.

00:04:05.892 --> 00:04:10.478
Nightly, run hardware tests, including power cuts during updates.

00:04:10.878 --> 00:04:15.824
When something breaks, start a bisect between a known bad and a known good commit.

00:04:16.224 --> 00:04:20.624
And let git bisect run your test automatically, to find the exact commit.

00:04:21.824 --> 00:04:27.420
Question for you: The new firmware fails to boot on one bar. What should happen automatically?

00:04:32.720 --> 00:04:39.953
Answer: The bootloader counts failed boot attempts and switches back to the previous, known-good copy of the system.

00:04:41.053 --> 00:04:41.749
Recap.

00:04:42.149 --> 00:04:45.377
Verify before switching, with a boot counter and fallback.

00:04:45.777 --> 00:04:49.028
Mark the slot successful only after real health checks.

00:04:49.428 --> 00:04:54.234
Test in layers: unit, virtual, hardware, certification, telemetry.

00:04:54.634 --> 00:04:58.291
Fix flaky tests, because they hide real regressions.

00:04:58.691 --> 00:05:00.897
And bisect with automated tests.
